Z||000009||84Bill||06-22-2001||09:59 AM||||You should go back to where you found it and look around a bit more. Where there is 1 there usually is a boatload more. I'd look more up hill and or up stream of where you got this. If you do find more call your local museum curator or university paleontology dept and let them know.||||reg||14
Z||000010||Steve Normington||06-22-2001||10:47 AM||||Make sure you have dibs if anything hatches.||||reg||1
Z||000011||TRiAD||06-22-2001||08:38 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Steve Normington:<BR><B>Make sure you have dibs if anything hatches.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Funny thing...that's actually a paelentology joke  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Yup, that's a Dino egg...from what I can see. <BR>i would look around <I>carefully</I> where you found it for more, then take it (and any others) to a museum. <BR>They'll make you an offer (it will be lower than you think, unless it's a rare species, but that's hard to tell), but don't sell it. Just allow thm to display it, but retain ownership yourself. <P>Nice find!<P>~Michael<P>||||reg||1
